Java Memory Management — Final Revision Notes
1. First understand the big picture

When a Java program runs, the JVM manages memory for different purposes.

For your course, remember these 4 main JVM memory areas:

Memory Area	Main Purpose
Method Area	Class information, method information/code, type information, static/class-level information
Heap	Objects and their instance variables
Stack	Method calls, stack frames, parameters, local variables and local references
PC Register	Keeps track of the current instruction being executed
Easy memory trick

Method Area = Class information
Heap = Objects
Stack = Method execution
PC = Current instruction

2. Method Area

The Method Area contains information related to classes.

For example:

class Student {

    int age;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

Conceptually, the Method Area contains information such as:

Student class
│
├── Field: age
│   └── Type: int
│
└── Method: display()
    └── Method code

So the Method Area knows:

There is a class called Student
It has a field called age
age has type int
It has a method called display()
Information/code associated with display() exists
Important

The method code does NOT move into the Stack when the method is called.

The method code remains associated with the class.

The Stack only gets a stack frame for that particular execution.

3. Heap

The Heap is mainly where objects are created.

Whenever you use:

new

you normally create an object on the Heap.

Example:

Student s = new Student();

Conceptually:

STACK                         HEAP

s ────────────────────────→  Student object
                              age = 20

Here:

s → reference variable
Student object → actual object
age = 20 → instance variable/value inside that object
4. Stack

The Stack is used for method execution.

Whenever a method is called, a stack frame is created for that particular method call.

Example:

void display(int marks) {

    int x = 50;

}

When display(90) is called:

STACK

display() frame
----------------
marks = 90
x = 50
----------------

When the method finishes, that frame is removed.

Important

A stack frame belongs to one particular method invocation.

For example:

display(90);
display(80);

The same method code is used twice, but there are separate executions/frames.

Conceptually:

METHOD AREA
    display() method code
          ↓
     same code reused

STACK
    display(90) frame

STACK
    display(80) frame
5. PC Register

PC means Program Counter.

It keeps track of which instruction is currently being executed by a thread.

Very simplified:

PC Register
     ↓
Current instruction

You normally don't directly work with the PC Register in Java programs.

For exams:

PC Register stores/keeps track of the address or location of the current instruction being executed.

6. What is a variable?

A variable is a named storage location used to hold a value or reference.

Example:

int age = 20;

Here:

age → variable name
int → declared type
20  → actual value

Don't imagine these as three separate objects.

Conceptually:

age : int = 20
7. Where is the type of a variable stored?

This was one of your important doubts.

Consider:

class Student {
    int age = 20;
}

The JVM/class metadata needs to know:

Student
│
└── age → int

So, in the simplified course model:

The declared type information is part of class/method metadata associated with the Method Area.

But the actual value belonging to a particular object is stored with that object.

METHOD AREA / CLASS METADATA

Student
└── age → int

Then after:

Student s = new Student();

Heap conceptually contains:

HEAP

Student object
└── age = 20
Therefore
age is int
      ↓
Class/type information
      ↓
Method Area / class metadata

age = 20
      ↓
Actual instance value
      ↓
Heap
8. Instance Variable

An instance variable is a variable declared inside a class but outside methods, without static.

Example:

class Student {

    int age = 20;
    String name = "Shobhit";

}

Both are instance variables:

age
name
Where are they stored?

The actual instance fields are stored inside each object on the Heap.

Example:

Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();

Conceptually:

HEAP

Student object 1
----------------
age = 20
name → "Shobhit"


Student object 2
----------------
age = 20
name → "Shobhit"

Each object gets its own copy of instance variables.

9. Why can't an instance variable exist without an object?

Suppose:

class Student {

    int age = 20;

}

The class definition tells the JVM:

Every Student object should have:

age : int

But there is no actual instance field storage until an object is created.

When:

Student s = new Student();

is executed:

HEAP

Student object
└── age = 20

So remember:

Class defines the instance variable; object provides the actual instance storage.

10. Static Variable

A static variable belongs to the class, not to individual objects.

Example:

class Student {

    static int count = 10;

}

There is only one shared count for the class.

Conceptually:

METHOD AREA / CLASS-LEVEL STORAGE

Student
└── static count = 10

If we create:

Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();

we don't get:

s1 → count
s2 → count

as separate copies.

Instead:

             Student class
                  │
                  ↓
           static count = 10
             ↗          ↖
           s1            s2

Both objects/classes access the same static variable.

Exam-level answer

Static variables are class-level variables and are commonly taught as being stored in the Method Area.

Technical note: Modern JVM implementations are more nuanced about the exact physical placement, but the above is the standard simplified model used for learning JVM memory.

11. Local Variable

A local variable is declared inside a method/block.

Example:

void display() {

    int x = 50;

}

x is a local variable.

When display() runs:

STACK

display() frame
----------------
x = 50
----------------

So:

Local variable → Stack frame

When the method finishes:

display() frame

is removed, so its local variables are no longer part of that active frame.

12. Parameter

A parameter is a variable declared in the method definition.

Example:

void display(int marks) {

}

marks is a parameter.

When:

s.display(90);

is called:

STACK

display() frame
----------------
marks = 90
----------------

So, at the course level:

Parameter → Stack frame

13. Local Reference Variable

This is where an important distinction comes in.

Consider:

Student s = new Student();

Here s is a reference variable, but it is also a local variable if written inside a method.

Therefore:

STACK
s ──────────────→ HEAP
                  Student object

So:

The local reference s is in the Stack frame, while the object it refers to is in the Heap.

14. Reference Variable vs Object

This was another major doubt.

Consider:

Student s = new Student();

There are two different things:

1. Reference variable
s
2. Object
new Student()

Conceptually:

STACK                         HEAP

s ─────────────────────────→ Student object
                              age = 20
                              name → "Shobhit"
Never confuse them
s ≠ object

s is a reference.

new Student() creates the object.

15. Can we make an object of a variable?

No.

A variable and an object are different concepts.

For example:

int age = 20;

age is a variable containing a primitive value.

You don't create an "object of age".

But:

Student s = new Student();

creates a Student object.

The variable s refers to that object.

16. What about String?

Consider:

String name = "Shobhit";

There are two things to understand:

name

and

"Shobhit"

If name is a local variable:

STACK

name ─────────────→ "Shobhit" String object

If name is an instance field:

class Student {
    String name = "Shobhit";
}

then:

HEAP

Student object
└── name ─────→ "Shobhit" String object

So the reference field is part of the object, while the String object itself is also on the Heap.

String literals have additional JVM/string-pool behavior, but for basic memory management remember:

String is an object/reference type, unlike int, which is primitive.

17. Primitive vs Reference Variable
Primitive
int age = 20;

Conceptually:

age : int = 20

There is no separate Integer object here.

Reference
String name = "Shobhit";

Conceptually:

name ─────→ String object

So:

Code	Variable	What it contains
int x = 50;	primitive variable	primitive value
String s = "Delhi";	reference variable	reference to object
Student s = new Student();	reference variable	reference to Student object
18. Instance Reference Variable

Consider:

class Student {

    String name = "Shobhit";

}

name is an instance variable and its type is String.

Conceptually:

METHOD AREA / CLASS METADATA

Student
└── name → String

After:

Student s = new Student();

Heap:

HEAP

Student object
└── name ─────────→ "Shobhit"

So:

name → reference
"Shobhit" → object
