21. Instance Method

Example:

class Student {

    int age = 20;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

display() is an instance method because it is not static.

Its method information/code is associated with the class:

METHOD AREA

Student
└── display() method code

When called:

Student s = new Student();
s.display();

a stack frame is created:

STACK

display() frame
----------------
this ─────────→ Student object
----------------

And:

HEAP

Student object
└── age = 20
22. What is this?

This is very important for instance methods.

Consider:

class Student {

    int age = 20;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

When:

s.display();

runs, the method needs to know:

"Which Student object's age should I use?"

The answer is the current object, represented by:

this

Conceptually:

STACK

display() frame

this ─────────────→ Student object
                         │
                         ↓
                      age = 20

Therefore:

System.out.println(age);

is conceptually like:

System.out.println(this.age);
23. Why does an instance method need an object?

Because it can access instance variables.

Example:

class Student {

    int age = 20;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

This is valid:

Student s = new Student();
s.display();

Because:

s
 ↓
Student object
 ↓
age = 20

But this doesn't make sense:

Student.display();

because there is no particular Student object/current this.

24. Static Method

Example:

class Student {

    static void show() {

        int x = 50;

        System.out.println(x);
    }
}

show() is a static method.

Its method code is associated with the class:

METHOD AREA

Student
└── show() method code

When:

Student.show();

runs:

STACK

show() frame
----------------
x = 50
----------------

There is no this reference.

Why?

Because a static method belongs to the class and does not operate on one particular Student object.

25. Static vs Instance Method

This is one of the most important comparisons.

	Instance Method	Static Method
Method code	Method Area/class-level	Method Area/class-level
Stack frame when called	Yes	Yes
this	Yes	No
Requires object to call normally	Yes	No
Can directly access instance variables	Yes	No
Can access static variables	Yes	Yes
Example
class Student {

    int age = 20;
    static int count = 10;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }

    static void showCount() {
        System.out.println(count);
    }
}

Conceptually:

METHOD AREA
────────────────────────
Student class

age → int
count → int (static)

display() code
showCount() code
────────────────────────

When display() runs:

STACK
display() frame

this ─────→ Student object

When showCount() runs:

STACK
showCount() frame

NO this
26. Why does Stack exist if methods are already in Method Area?

This was one of your biggest doubts.

The answer is:

Method Area stores the method itself. Stack stores the execution of that method.

Think of it like a recipe.

Method Area
display() recipe/code

The recipe exists once.

Stack

When someone actually follows the recipe:

display() execution
marks = 90
x = 50
city = "Delhi"

Each execution gets its own frame.

Easy line to remember

Method Area = What the method is
Stack = What happens when the method runs

27. Complete Example

Now let's put everything together.

class Student {

    static int count = 10;

    int age = 20;

    String name = "Shobhit";

    void display(int marks) {

        int x = 50;

        String city = "Delhi";

        Student s2 = new Student();

        System.out.println(age);
        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(marks);
        System.out.println(x);
        System.out.println(city);
    }
}

class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s = new Student();

        s.display(90);
    }
}

Let's execute it step-by-step.

28. Step 1 — Class Loading

The JVM loads information about Student.

Conceptually:

METHOD AREA / CLASS METADATA
────────────────────────────────

Student

Fields:
    count → int → static
    age   → int
    name  → String

Methods:
    display(int)

display:
    parameter marks → int
    local variables:
        x → int
        city → String
        s2 → Student

method code / bytecode
Important correction

The actual local variables are not sitting in the Method Area waiting to be used.

Their runtime values belong to the active Stack frame.

Information about local variables may also appear in bytecode/debug metadata when available, but don't confuse that with the actual runtime storage.

29. Step 2 — main() Starts

The JVM starts executing:

main(String[] args)

A main() stack frame is created.

Conceptually:

STACK

main() frame
-----------------------
args → String[] object
-----------------------

The args reference points to an array object on the Heap.

30. Step 3 — Create Student Object

This line executes:

Student s = new Student();

First:

new Student()

creates an object on the Heap.

Conceptually:

HEAP

Student object
----------------
count → shared/static
age = 20
name ─────→ "Shobhit"
----------------

But remember:

count is static, so don't think of it as a separate copy inside every Student object. The simplified model places its shared class-level storage separately.

Then:

s

is the local reference in the main() frame.

So:

STACK                         HEAP

main() frame

s ─────────────────────────→ Student object
                              age = 20
                              name ───→ "Shobhit"
31. Step 4 — Call display(90)

This executes:

s.display(90);

The JVM starts executing the method.

A new stack frame is created:

STACK

display() frame
------------------------
this ─────→ Student object
marks = 90
------------------------

main() frame
------------------------
s ────────→ Student object
------------------------

Notice something important:

Both:

this

and:

s

refer to the same Student object.

32. Step 5 — Local Variable x

Inside display():

int x = 50;

x is local.

So:

STACK

display() frame
------------------------
this ─────→ Student object
marks = 90
x = 50
------------------------

The value 50 is part of the local variable's runtime storage in the frame.

33. Step 6 — Local String Reference

Next:

String city = "Delhi";

city is a local reference variable.

Conceptually:

STACK

display() frame
------------------------
this ─────→ Student object
marks = 90
x = 50
city ─────→ "Delhi"
------------------------

The String object is on the Heap/string pool.

34. Step 7 — Create Another Student

Next:

Student s2 = new Student();

A second Student object is created.

STACK

display() frame
------------------------
this ─────→ Student object 1
marks = 90
x = 50
city ─────→ "Delhi"
s2 ──────→ Student object 2
------------------------

Heap:

HEAP

Student object 1
----------------
age = 20
name ───→ "Shobhit"
