It depends on what kind of variable it is.

Local reference
Student s = new Student();
STACK
s ─────→ HEAP
Instance reference
class Student {
    String name;
}
HEAP

Student object
└── name ─────→ String object
Parameter reference
void display(Student s)
STACK

display() frame
└── s ─────→ Student object
Static reference
static Student s;

It belongs to class-level/static storage.

38. One Very Important Rule

Don't say:

"The variable is always in Stack."

That is wrong.

Because variables can be:

Instance variable → Heap
Static variable   → Class-level storage
Local variable    → Stack
Parameter         → Stack
39. One Very Important Rule About Objects

Don't say:

"Reference variable and object are stored in the same place."

Usually, for a local reference:

Student s = new Student();

they are conceptually in different places:

STACK                         HEAP

s ─────────────────────────→ Student object

The reference is in the Stack.

The object is in the Heap.

40. One Very Important Rule About Methods

Don't say:

"When a method is called, the method moves from Method Area to Stack."

That's incorrect.

Instead:

METHOD AREA
display() method code
       │
       │ called
       ↓
STACK
display() stack frame

The code remains class-level.

The Stack gets a new execution frame.

