42. Why Can an Instance Method Access an Instance Variable?

Example:

class Student {

    int age = 20;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

When:

Student s = new Student();
s.display();

runs:

STACK

display() frame
    this
      │
      ↓
HEAP

Student object
    age = 20

Inside the method:

age

is conceptually:

this.age

Therefore the JVM knows which object's age to access.

43. What Happens If There Is No Student Object?

Suppose:

class Student {

    int age = 20;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

Before:

new Student();

there is no actual Student object.

Therefore there is no actual:

age = 20

instance field belonging to an object.

But class metadata still knows:

Student
└── age → int

After:

Student s = new Student();

the actual field exists inside the newly created object:

HEAP

Student object
└── age = 20
